Description: GNOME as a phone (metapackage)
 GNOME Phone turns a stock GNOME Shell into a phone shell: the Neo launcher as
 the home screen, Android-style gesture navigation, recents, the status bar,
 the notification shade with quick settings, the lock screen and the always-on
 display, and a set of phone apps.
 .
 This metapackage installs the shell extension, the launcher and the system
 helpers, and recommends the session and the apps.

Package: gnome-shell-extension-gnome-phone
Architecture: all
Depends: gnome-shell (>= 51~),
         gnome-shell (<< 52~),
         neo-launcher (>= ${source:Version}),
         gnome-phone-daemons (>= ${source:Version}),
         python3,
         gir1.2-gnomebluetooth-3.0,
         gir1.2-gnomedesktop-4.0,
         gir1.2-gudev-1.0,
         gir1.2-nm-1.0,
         ${misc:Depends},
Recommends: feedbackd,
            iio-sensor-proxy,
            gir1.2-manette-0.2,
Suggests: gnome-phone-session,
Description: GNOME Shell extension that makes the shell a phone
 The gnome-phone@yesman.de extension replaces GNOME Shell's top panel,
 overview and lock screen with a phone's: Android-style gesture navigation
 (home, back, recents), the status bar, the notification shade with quick
 settings, heads-up notifications, the volume panel, the power menu, the
 lock screen with PIN or pattern, and the always-on display. The Neo launcher
 is started as the home screen.
 .
 The extension is for GNOME Shell 51. It is not turned on for the regular
 GNOME session; the gnome-phone-session package provides a "GNOME Phone"
 session that always runs it.

Package: gnome-phone-session
Architecture: all
Depends: gnome-session (>= 51~),
         gnome-shell-extension-gnome-phone (= ${source:Version}),
         ${misc:Depends},
Suggests: gdm3,
Description: GNOME Phone session
 A "GNOME Phone" session for display managers: GNOME's own session with GNOME
 Shell in the gnome-phone mode, which always runs the gnome-phone extension.
 .
 Also systemd units for a phone without a display manager (gnome-phone.service
 starts the session on tty7 as one user), and neo-dock, which switches to the
 regular GNOME desktop while an external display is connected. None of these
 units is enabled on installation; see README.Debian.

Package: gnome-phone-daemons
Architecture: any
Section: admin
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
Description: GNOME Phone system helpers
 Small system programs used by the GNOME Phone shell and apps:
  * gp-auth checks the lock screen's PIN or pattern through PAM (the PIN or
    pattern is the user's own password);
  * neo-wakeup wakes the phone from suspend at the times apps ask for (alarm
    clocks, calendar reminders), over D-Bus (de.yesman.Wakeup), and publishes
    the next alarm clock;
  * input-boost raises the GPU's (and optionally the CPUs') clock floor while
    the touchscreen is touched. It is not enabled by default: its floor must
    be tuned for the device.

Package: sim-apn
Architecture: all
Section: net
Depends: python3,
         python3-gi,
         gir1.2-nm-1.0,
         mobile-broadband-provider-info,
         ${misc:Depends},
Recommends: modemmanager,
            network-manager,
Description: mobile data profile for every new SIM
 sim-apn watches ModemManager for SIMs that no NetworkManager connection
 applies to, and adds one for each: named after the operator, its APN from
 mobile-broadband-provider-info, pinned to that SIM, with roaming off. A
 connection made by hand, and a SIM that already has one, are left alone.

Package: neo-launcher
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
Recommends: mesa-vulkan-drivers,
Description: Neo, a phone home screen in Vulkan
 Neo is the home screen of GNOME Phone: pages with a dock, an app drawer that
 follows the finger, widgets, icon packs and icon shapes, and parallax
 wallpapers. It is a Wayland client drawn with Vulkan 1.0 and scrolls with
 libspongy's Android physics.
 .
 It is started by the gnome-phone GNOME Shell extension, which makes its
 window the desktop.

Package: libspongy-1-0
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: same
Section: libs
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
Description: Android scroll physics (stretch overscroll, fling, springs)
 libspongy reimplements Android's scrolling physics in C: the stretch
 overscroll (EdgeEffect), the fling (OverScroller), velocity tracking,
 spring animations and the interpolators, to the numbers of Android's own.
 .
 This package contains the shared library.

Package: libspongy-gtk-1-0
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: same
Section: libs
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
Description: GTK 4 scroll view with Android's stretch overscroll
 SpongyScrollView is a GTK 4 scrolled window that flings and stretches the
 way Android's lists do, on libspongy's physics.
 .
 This package contains the shared library.

